`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
The tell here wasn't "files are missing" — it was "`git status` is clean but the tracker disagrees." A dirty working tree would have meant "implemented but not committed" (the `.2` bead's situation last session). A *clean* tree plus a claimed implementation means the code must already be committed somewhere; the only question is which commit. `git log --oneline -- <path>` answers that directly by walking history filtered to specific files, sidestepping any need to trust a commit's own message.

This is also why `dedupStage` moving earlier in the pipeline matters architecturally: GitLab's GraphQL API doesn't offer a "list minus discussions but still dedup-aware" primitive — the fan-out across sources (author, reviewer) is inherently going to surface the same MR multiple times before the adapter can know that. Doing the expensive discussions fetch (`enrichStage`) *after* collapsing duplicates, rather than before, is the whole latency win: it's O(unique MRs) instead of O(MR × sources-that-reference-it).
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

